# Web Development: Web Components

## 1. What Are Web Components?

Web Components are a set of browser technologies that allow developers to create **custom, reusable HTML elements** with their own behavior and appearance.

For example, instead of writing the same profile card markup repeatedly, you can create your own element:

```html
<user-card></user-card>
```

The browser can render this custom element using JavaScript that you define.

Web Components are built around three main technologies:

- **Custom Elements:** Define your own HTML elements.
- **Shadow DOM:** Encapsulate a component's DOM and styles.
- **HTML Templates:** Define markup that can be reused.

## 2. Why Are Web Components Useful?

Web Components help developers:

- Reuse UI elements across multiple pages.
- Organize interfaces into independent components.
- Encapsulate styles to reduce unintended CSS conflicts.
- Build components that work without a specific JavaScript framework.
- Share UI components across different projects.

## 3. Create Your First Custom Element

Let's create a reusable greeting element.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Web Components</title>
</head>
<body>

    <hello-world></hello-world>

    <script>
        class HelloWorld extends HTMLElement {
            connectedCallback() {
                this.textContent = "Hello, Web Components!";
            }
        }

        customElements.define("hello-world", HelloWorld);
    </script>

</body>
</html>
```

**Output:**

Hello, Web Components!

### How does it work?

1. `HTMLElement` is the base class for HTML elements.
2. `HelloWorld` defines the behavior of our custom element.
3. `connectedCallback()` runs when the element is connected to the document.
4. `customElements.define()` registers the custom element with the browser.

Custom element names must contain a hyphen, which is why we use `hello-world` instead of `helloworld`.

## 4. Understanding the Shadow DOM

Normally, CSS rules can affect many elements throughout a page. The Shadow DOM allows a component to encapsulate its internal DOM structure and styles.

Here's an example:

```html
<user-card></user-card>

<script>
    class UserCard extends HTMLElement {
        constructor() {
            super();

            const shadow = this.attachShadow({
                mode: "open"
            });

            shadow.innerHTML = `
                <style>
                    .card {
                        padding: 16px;
                        border: 1px solid #ccc;
                        border-radius: 8px;
                    }

                    h2 {
                        color: blue;
                    }
                </style>

                <div class="card">
                    <h2>Nikitha</h2>
                    <p>Computer Science Student</p>
                </div>
            `;
        }
    }

    customElements.define("user-card", UserCard);
</script>
```

The component creates its own shadow root and places its markup and styles inside it.

This helps prevent ordinary page CSS selectors from accidentally styling the component's internal elements.

**Important:** Shadow DOM provides encapsulation, not security. An open shadow root can be accessed through `element.shadowRoot`.

## 5. Understanding HTML Templates

The `<template>` element stores HTML that is not rendered immediately. JavaScript can clone its contents whenever a component needs them.

```html
<template id="card-template">
    <article>
        <h3>Reusable Card</h3>
        <p>This content comes from a template.</p>
    </article>
</template>

<div id="container"></div>

<script>
    const template = document.getElementById("card-template");
    const container = document.getElementById("container");

    container.appendChild(
        template.content.cloneNode(true)
    );
</script>
```

The template content is cloned and inserted into the container.

Templates are useful when the same markup needs to be created multiple times.

## 6. Custom Elements vs Framework Components

| Feature | Web Components | Framework components |
|---|---|---|
| Browser support | Built into modern browsers | Depends on the framework and its runtime |
| Custom HTML elements | Yes | Framework-specific syntax may be used |
| Style encapsulation | Available through Shadow DOM | Depends on the framework and styling approach |
| Framework dependency | Not required | Usually requires the chosen framework |
| Reusability | Across framework environments with suitable integration | Often centered on the framework's ecosystem |

Web Components and frameworks are not competitors in every situation. Framework applications can use Web Components, and Web Components can be used without a framework.

## 7. Important Lifecycle Callbacks

Custom elements provide lifecycle callbacks that run at specific stages.

| Callback | When it runs |
|---|---|
| `connectedCallback()` | When the element is connected to the document |
| `disconnectedCallback()` | When the element is disconnected |
| `adoptedCallback()` | When the element is moved to a different document |
| `attributeChangedCallback()` | When a configured observed attribute changes |

For example, `disconnectedCallback()` can be used to clean up event listeners or other resources when a component is removed.

## 8. Common Mistakes

- Creating a custom element name without a hyphen.
- Forgetting to register the element using `customElements.define()`.
- Assuming all CSS automatically becomes isolated without using Shadow DOM.
- Treating an open shadow root as a security boundary.
- Forgetting to clean up resources when a component is removed.
- Assuming Web Components automatically provide application state management or routing.

## 9. Key Takeaways

- Web Components let you build reusable custom HTML elements.
- Custom Elements define the element and its lifecycle.
- Shadow DOM encapsulates a component's internal structure and styles.
- HTML Templates provide reusable markup.
- Web Components work without requiring a specific JavaScript framework.
- They can be integrated into larger applications when appropriate.

## One-Line Summary

**Web Components use browser-native technologies to create reusable, encapsulated UI elements that can work across different web projects.**
